d key succeeded pre-restart, and the key persisted unchanged (single entry) through a full node restart, confirmed again post-restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4818a113da65415f\",\"cmd-0b963ad66903422d\",\"cmd-3addd321d2c248a8\",\"cmd-3da3e95f298c4a22\",\"cmd-6047dd19026f4ae1\",\"cmd-adc7ce5584554f75\"]},{\"id\":\"key-only-ssh-auth-required\",\"requirement\":\"SSH on node1 requires key-based authentication (password/interactive auth disabled)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A drop-in (10-key-only.conf) sets PubkeyAuthentication yes / PasswordAuthentication no / KbdInteractiveAuthentication no / AuthenticationMethods publickey; sshd -t validated and sshd -T confirms the effective runtime config both before and after a full restart. Live SSH tests directly demonstrate the behavior: an attempt with no matching key is rejected with 'Permission denied (publickey)' only, a matching-key attempt succeeds, and a password-preferred attempt is rejected, all repeated post-restart. opsadmin's password hash is also locked ('!'), reinforcing that no password path exists.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-77405deb63074b7f\",\"cmd-637eec4009ea48fd\",\"cmd-b5ad83ac28c64971\",\"cmd-5d11f417e00c411c\",\"cmd-ff56e0b47d9140c7\",\"cmd-fe25d8aa3268431e\",\"cmd-4756badc9c6343f2\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-recovery-evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: pre-restart healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/config state on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart baseline captured boot_id 7ef78016... with sshd active and opsadmin locked. shutdown -r now was issued and acknowledged before disconnect; subsequent polls show connection timeout/reset (unreachable), then a successful poll with a new boot_id (bdd30bd8...) and new uptime. Post-restart checks confirm sshd active, opsadmin still locked, authorized_keys and sshd_config.d/10-key-only.conf intact, sshd -T unchanged, and live SSH key-auth/password-rejection retested successfully. This is corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots (before_prepare boot-identity 7ef78016... vs after_executor boot-identity bdd30bd8...).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c2834d238f4d4c15\",\"cmd-85be8b4c8db342ec\",\"cmd-792ae0efe61149e8\",\"cmd-7cd8628b03d14281\",\"cmd-0a20c3c86ce049d0\",\"cmd-363f96d5f2eb42c6\",\"cmd-adc7ce5584554f75\",\"cmd-fe25d8aa3268431e\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Executor actions were tightly scoped to the task: user/key creation, sshd hardening drop-in, and password lock, all with a clear causal role in satisfying the two material requirements. Two temporary SSH keypairs (cmd-5d11f417e00c411c and cmd-fe25d8aa3268431e) were added to authorized_keys purely to prove login works, then promptly removed and the file restored to the single required key (cmd-c97739f0f5404bc1, cmd-3addd321d2c248a8, cmd-1eed4db013be49da) — bounded, low-risk, fully reverted verification steps directly supporting the outcome. One minor residue: /tmp/reboot.log, created as a redirect target for the backgrounded 'shutdown -r now' command (cmd-85be8b4c8db342ec), was left on disk after restart per the after_executor temporary-files snapshot; this is a trivial, non-sensitive log file with negligible blast radius but was not cleaned up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5d11f417e00c411c\",\"cmd-c97739f0f5404bc1\",\"cmd-fe25d8aa3268431e\",\"cmd-1eed4db013be49da\",\"cmd-85be8b4c8db342ec\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task — creating opsadmin with the specified public key and enforcing key-only SSH authentication — are directly and repeatedly demonstrated via live SSH tests, config validation, and effective-config queries, both before and after a full node restart. The restart evidence protocol is well executed: a clean pre-restart baseline, an acknowledged reboot, an observed unreachable window, a changed boot_id, and full post-restart recovery of the user, key, and sshd configuration, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The only blemish is a leftover /tmp/reboot.log file from the reboot command, a trivial and low-risk residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"Live SSH verification was performed from the node itself (loopback, 127.0.0.1) via the executor's shell rather than from an external client, so external-network-path SSH behavior was not directly observed, though this does not affect the authentication-policy conclusions.\",\"No requirement in the task instruction called for locking opsadmin's password, but this is a reasonable supporting hardening step with no negative side effects for the key-only requirement.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"opsadmin-key-configured","requirement":"On node1, opsadmin exists with the specified ed25519 public key configured for SSH access (as authorized key)","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin did not previously exist; it was created with home/.ssh (0700) and authorized_keys (0600) containing exactly the required key with correct SELinux context (ssh_home_t). Live SSH login with the required key succeeded pre-restart, and the key persisted unchanged (single entry) through a full node restart, confirmed again post-restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4818a113da65415f","cmd-0b963ad66903422d","cmd-3addd321d2c248a8","cmd-3da3e95f298c4a22","cmd-6047dd19026f4ae1","cmd-adc7ce5584554f75"]},{"id":"key-only-ssh-auth-required","requirement":"SSH on node1 requires key-based authentication (password/interactive auth disabled)","status":"satisfied","summary":"A drop-in (10-key-only.conf) sets PubkeyAuthentication yes / PasswordAuthentication no / KbdInteractiveAuthentication no / AuthenticationMethods publickey; sshd -t validated and sshd -T confirms the effective runtime config both before and after a full restart. Live SSH tests directly demonstrate the behavior: an attempt with no matching key is rejected with 'Permission denied (publickey)' only, a matching-key attempt succeeds, and a password-preferred attempt is rejected, all repeated post-restart. opsadmin's password hash is also locked ('!'), reinforcing that no password path exists.","evidence_ids":["cmd-77405deb63074b7f","cmd-637eec4009ea48fd","cmd-b5ad83ac28c64971","cmd-5d11f417e00c411c","cmd-ff56e0b47d9140c7","cmd-fe25d8aa3268431e","cmd-4756badc9c6343f2"]},{"id":"restart-recovery-evidence","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: pre-restart healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/config state on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart baseline captured boot_id 7ef78016... with sshd active and opsadmin locked. shutdown -r now was issued and acknowledged before disconnect; subsequent polls show connection timeout/reset (unreachable), then a successful poll with a new boot_id (bdd30bd8...) and new uptime. Post-restart checks confirm sshd active, opsadmin still locked, authorized_keys and sshd_config.d/10-key-only.conf intact, sshd -T unchanged, and live SSH key-auth/password-rejection retested successfully. This is corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots (before_prepare boot-identity 7ef78016... vs after_executor boot-identity bdd30bd8...).","evidence_ids":["cmd-c2834d238f4d4c15","cmd-85be8b4c8db342ec","cmd-792ae0efe61149e8","cmd-7cd8628b03d14281","cmd-0a20c3c86ce049d0","cmd-363f96d5f2eb42c6","cmd-adc7ce5584554f75","cmd-fe25d8aa3268431e"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Executor actions were tightly scoped to the task: user/key creation, sshd hardening drop-in, and password lock, all with a clear causal role in satisfying the two material requirements. Two temporary SSH keypairs (cmd-5d11f417e00c411c and cmd-fe25d8aa3268431e) were added to authorized_keys purely to prove login works, then promptly removed and the file restored to the single required key (cmd-c97739f0f5404bc1, cmd-3addd321d2c248a8, cmd-1eed4db013be49da) — bounded, low-risk, fully reverted verification steps directly supporting the outcome. One minor residue: /tmp/reboot.log, created as a redirect target for the backgrounded 'shutdown -r now' command (cmd-85be8b4c8db342ec), was left on disk after restart per the after_executor temporary-files snapshot; this is a trivial, non-sensitive log file with negligible blast radius but was not cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5d11f417e00c411c","cmd-c97739f0f5404bc1","cmd-fe25d8aa3268431e","cmd-1eed4db013be49da","cmd-85be8b4c8db342ec","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes of the task — creating opsadmin with the specified public key and enforcing key-only SSH authentication — are directly and repeatedly demonstrated via live SSH tests, config validation, and effective-config queries, both before and after a full node restart. The restart evidence protocol is well executed: a clean pre-restart baseline, an acknowledged reboot, an observed unreachable window, a changed boot_id, and full post-restart recovery of the user, key, and sshd configuration, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The only blemish is a leftover /tmp/reboot.log file from the reboot command, a trivial and low-risk residue.","limitations":["Live SSH verification was performed from the node itself (loopback, 127.0.0.1) via the executor's shell rather than from an external client, so external-network-path SSH behavior was not directly observed, though this does not affect the authentication-policy conclusions.","No requirement in the task instruction called for locking opsadmin's password, but this is a reasonable supporting hardening step with no negative side effects for the key-only requirement."]},"ttft_ms":11411,"type":"result","duration_ms":48420,"uuid":"6bc39f64-ba84-4f37-9e67-7a16e56e17fc","ttft_stream_ms":1999,"time_to_request_ms":187,"first_content_frame_ms":1999,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
